WHAT WAS WRONG
The estimate was loanAmount x latestRate: a full year of interest
regardless of drawdown date, at whichever rate was most recent, with the
offset account ignored despite already being in the schema.

MEASURED IMPACT (cross-checked in SQL against the user's own example,
loan from 27 Oct 2025, FY2025-26, $500k at 6%):
  days in window           247 of 365
  old full-year estimate   $30,000.00
  new prorated estimate    $20,301.37   (32% overstatement removed)
  with a $100k offset      $16,241.10
The TypeScript helper and Postgres agree to the cent.

DESIGN DECISIONS
- Rate segments rather than a single rate. The opening rate is the latest
  one effective on or before charging begins; later ones split the window.
  Rates effective before the loan started are correctly ignored as
  boundaries but still supply the opening rate.
- Offset netted off the principal, floored at zero, so an offset larger
  than the loan yields zero interest rather than a negative deduction.
- Inclusive day counts, and daysInFy for the denominator, so a leap year
  divides by 366.
- Only start_date was added, not an end date: a discharged loan drops out
  of the balance, and an end date would imply a repayment history this
  table does not hold.
- NOT amortised. Repayment type is unknown, and assuming P&I would be a
  guess. assumedConstantBalance is returned so the UI states the
  limitation rather than leaving the reader to assume precision.

KNOWN LIMITATION (disclosed, not hidden)
property_offset_accounts.balance is a CURRENT balance with no effective
date, so applying it to a past financial year is an approximation. That is
acceptable for a figure explicitly excluded from claims, but it is the
reason this remains an estimate rather than becoming claimable.

